the most important financial rule for  entrepreneurs so got a great question  from one of my youtube viewers love you  guys  Albert wrote in the saying what tips are  financial discipline should I adopt as  an upcoming entrepreneur this is a great  question and Albert the number one the  most important financial rule that you  need to adopt as an entrepreneur is  don't spend money until you're making  money it's easy to spend money ok  anybody can spend money you are really  good at spending money right if I gave  you a million dollars today you could  find a million different ways to spend  money not everybody can make money and  that's the key difference as entrence we  don't start off with a lot of money ok  at least I didn't if you've got some  rich Uncle Joe who's willing to back  everything that you're doing and dump  all his money into your company then  fine do whatever you want but if money  is important and you don't have a lot of  it you guys it's got to be used properly  and wisely like it was for me then you  have to make sure that you're spending  it properly ok so a lot of entrepreneurs  do this say hey I'm gonna launch a  t-shirt company so I'm gonna go out and  I'm gonna buy a thousand t-shirts and  spend all my money on that and you end  up with a thousand t-shirts sitting in  your garage because you picked the wrong  product or I'm gonna go out and I'm  gonna start making videos and I'm gonna  get my own camera and I'm gonna buy all  the all the tools and then you get bored  of it after two weeks and you stop and  you got this equipment just sitting  there in the corner that's what a lot of  people do they spend whatever money they  have trying to get something to go but  it was the wrong vision it was the wrong  product it wasn't the right thing for  them and now you're out of money or  worse you go and you take out a loan to  try to make this thing happen maybe you  put an extra mortgage on your house and  now you're forcing this monthly payment  back that you can barely afford let me  give an example so with my with my  videos okay I make these videos right  you watch them every day hopefully when  I started my first videos were just with  my my DSLR camera  that's how I started I didn't want to  spend any money on it until I knew that  I had a way to make money and then I was  committed to keep on doing it and then  as I got a little bit of money from  advertising and as I saw some results  and I got a little bit of speaking gigs  here and there I could justify spending  more money on it and spending more time  and I knew I wasn't getting bored of it  because sometimes you start something  and then you get bored of it and you  invest all those money into it so it  started spinning a little bit more and I  started upgrading my camera and I kept  the cheap I went to Costco and my first  camera was gonna be 250 bucks 300 bucks  and I slowly started upgrading my  cameras through Costco and I always went  to Costco because you had 90 days to  test it out before we had to return it  whereas with the camera stores they only  gave you seven so if I didn't like it  and I didn't use it if I stopped having  an interest in it I could always just  take it back and get my money refunded  so I slowly started increasing my budget  as I started seeing results and I knew  it was working and I knew I want to  continue it so now how much have I spent  I spent $3,000 on my current gear setup  I have my camera I got my tripod I have  my microphone maybe a little over 3,000  I have Christina who edits my videos hey  Christina  I've got Monsour who does some research  for some of our video so I was a team  around me so I have salary to pay that  goes into making these videos happen but  this has happened because I've invested  along the way into making my product  better as I got results any company that  you start you can start small you can  bootstrap it up to prove that one you're  still interested in doing this and two  you find a way to make money at it if  you can get those two things happening  then it's easy to justify putting more  money into it okay putting any money  into it I think back to adventure  capital days and the easiest deals to  fund were the companies who just needed  expansion capital right so in other  words they'd already built up a business  they'd already proven their market maybe  they were only selling in their local  area and now they wanted  take it nationally and they needed  marketing dollars to do it that's the  easiest kind of money to raise and they  take the the least amount of equity from  you because it's not as risky right  because you've shown that it works you  shown that it works on a small scale and  now you want to build it out that's  worth spending some money on the hardest  money to raise is the R&amp;D the  early-stage the pure startup because  it's just an idea and who knows maybe  you'll maybe you'll get tired of it  maybe you'll try it for three months and  then decide now this isn't for you or  maybe you'll make a ton of mistakes  because you've never done this before  you haven't figured out the right  business model and you have to pivot and  pivot and pivot and pivot until you find  it and I don't want you wasting your  money all along that path so the number  one most important financial rule for  entrepreneurs is don't spend money until  you're making money believe those are  watching like the video you can support  me by clicking in the link in the  description below leave a comment let me  know what you think and click on my face  subscribe to the channel thank you so  much I'll see you soon
 

